#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176749} :activity/id #uuid "b7c1b63f-a8ec-54b2-8bd2-f993ffba0210" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553 :topic/id #uuid "fed8aeb7-cb56-5ca6-9352-ed88fd02f45e" :topic/math-academy-id 3636} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177001 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct the marginal mass functions for two discrete random variables from their joint probability mass function. We now wish to formulate analogous definitions when our random variables are continuous. \n\nFirst, recall that if $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of the *discrete* random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively, then\n\n- the marginal mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_X(x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y)\n$$\n\n- and the marginal mass function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are *continuous* random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y).$  \n\n- The **marginal density function** of $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n- The **marginal density function** of $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f (x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to a marginal density function as a **marginal probability density function** (or **marginal PDF**).\n\nIt's helpful to appreciate the analogy between the discrete and continuous cases as this often helps us to remember their definitions. In summary:\n\n- To convert from discrete to continuous, we turn sums into integrals.\n\n- When we compute $f_X$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $Y.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $y.$\n\n- When we compute $f_Y$ in the discrete case, we sum over all possible values of $X.$ In the continuous case, we integrate with respect to $x.$\n\nLet's now see a concrete example of how to compute a marginal density function." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0ae531f-9bb0-5ba9-bd88-40955c3b086f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5478 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5ee40682-9346-5226-8f31-7485c414635e" :step/math-academy-id 34136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175999 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175852 :question/id #uuid "ed593b5a-be86-5f9a-b9a5-d82b07ed2cfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2b5bc-ecf8-5e8e-bf4e-0cde666ec594" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476940}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Distribution From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "e1c872df-df0e-5ace-95d4-79d04118e90c" :step/math-academy-id 34284 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176000 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175853 :question/id #uuid "e0123816-b3f6-5fd4-a6ae-09c31bcce5cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9193"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc918d1c-7446-59a5-ad73-f705f83ae49c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476954}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability From a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "e005bf1f-d37f-59a9-8616-8c44dcccf27f" :step/math-academy-id 34287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176001 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175854 :question/id #uuid "acab7d19-e027-53cc-b491-58cbd73ee640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad789c4f-c4e1-5efc-8241-a55e4ac1dd07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476948}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal CDF"} :step/id #uuid "92b212c1-f8b5-5c20-9ba2-51519f5eb1c8" :step/math-academy-id 34294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176037 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175684 :question/id #uuid "b26c36dd-e371-5598-96a3-73fb1c147a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc2759a6-76b0-5410-b695-1e28b3168cb8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476947}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Distribution From a Joint Distribution: Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "79e0c173-47c4-57c7-89d0-44529d8a86f6" :step/math-academy-id 37919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177056 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/e2/e229c78e51036e7fb7b7e9345bbe4f2ede5704032d65d2581b53c8a5f59ef603.svg)\n\nLet's find the expression for the marginal density function $f_X(x)$ of $X.$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\leq x \\leq 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}]-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_X(x)$ is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e8eb5c2dedf51c1b232af9e2dd6a4eb12621daaa78ab34f18a46e26a9a6d2887.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3374b507-4cf9-507d-a469-7279c8a65dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6292 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "1d45cbe3-5cd1-5dd4-b04c-1c3e4790d561" :step/math-academy-id 38107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177057 :tutorial/content "Recall that the cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we know the marginal distributions $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ of the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\nThen we can define the **marginal cumulative distribution functions** (or **marginal CDFs**) of $X$ and $Y$ analogously as follows:\n\n- The marginal CDF of $X,$ denoted $F_X(x),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = P(X\\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\n- The marginal CDF of $Y,$ denoted $F_Y(y),$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = P(Y\\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y f_Y(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "cf432491-2aee-56d2-9488-c13965e4b0c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6293 :tutorial/title "Marginal Cumulative Distribution Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f9edd5a3-d60c-5e03-ab11-6e9add487f22" :step/math-academy-id 38110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176757}}] :activity/title "Marginal Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176153}]}
